`srmech.math.laplacian.propagate` IS the shipped Excite/Propagate/Harvest op
(harvest = e^{-zL}·u0, arg(z) the coherence dial), so the second question's
"is there a generator whose own equation enforces directionality" is decidable
against shipped code rather than in the abstract.

Measured on a 3-node path graph, L positive-semidefinite:

* z REAL (thermal) genuinely CONTRACTS — ‖·‖² falls 1.000000 -> 0.401414. The
  generator's sign condition is real and visible: L is PSD, so -L is
  dissipative and the forward flow is non-expanding.
* but z = -1 is ACCEPTED and inverts the forward map to 1.5e-15. Nothing
  restricts z to the closed right half-plane.

So the arrow is a property of the INPUT z, not of the operator's own equation.
The structural reason is finite dimension: every e^{-tL} is invertible
(det = e^{-t·tr L} != 0), so {e^{-tL} : t >= 0} always extends to a group. This
is the same fact the §9 shift census measures — on a finite carrier, injective
implies group. It also matches the standard characterisation exactly: a C_0
semigroup extends to a group iff S(t) is bijective for every t > 0
(Buehler & Salamon, Functional Analysis, ETH Zuerich lecture notes 8 June 2017,
Theorem 7.21(iii)).

Registry census: 0 of 649 ops match semigroup|monoid. The only shipped
proper monoid-not-group this found is (Z/12, srmech.math.cyclic.mod_mul) —
4 units of 12, absorbing 0, 6 |-> 0 — where information is destroyed and
cannot be recovered.

